#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093265} :activity/id #uuid "13d15497-8701-507f-9f1f-33e1d3fe3a8a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093265 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093492 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given two linear transformations represented by the matrices\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, $R$ represents a rotation of $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise about the origin, and $S$ represents a reflection in the $x$-axis. Now, let $\\mathbf{v}$ be a vector on the plane. Let's apply $R$ and then $S$ (in this particular order) to $\\mathbf{v}.$ We obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\overset{R}{⟶}Rv\\overset{S}{⟶}S(Rv)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above states that $\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $R$ is mapped to $R\\mathbf{v},$ and then $R\\mathbf{v}$ under the action of $S$ is mapped to $S(R\\mathbf{v}).$\n\nSince matrix multiplication is associative, we can write\n\n$$\nS(R\\mathbf{v}) = (SR)\\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, the product $SR$ (in this particular order) represents the combined transformation. \n\nIn our case, the matrix that represents the combined transformation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SR & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4a2a46b5-6f61-5de9-8f83-1b83fec624c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7815 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8e965334-5204-5e65-a55c-98aa1939d5f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093266}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093266 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093493 :tutorial/content "The order in which the multiplication is carried out is extremely important! \n\nFor example, consider the transformations \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nComputing the product $SR,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation $SR$ represents a reflection in line $y=-x.$ \n\nOn the other hand, computing the product $RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product $RS$ represents a reflection in line $y=x,$ which is a completely different transformation than $SR.$ \n\nIn general, the transformations are performed from right-to-left. So, for example, if we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf v' = SR\\mathbf v,\n$$\n\nthen we should think of the transformation $R$ as being applied *first*, followed by $S.$\n\n**Note:** The reason why transformations are performed right-to-left is that the rightmost transformation is the first transformation preceding the input. In our case, to compute $SR \\mathbf v,$\n\n- we first apply $R$ to $\\mathbf v$ to get $R \\mathbf v,$\n\n- and then we apply $S$ to $R \\mathbf v$ to get $SR \\mathbf v.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7124e650-700a-576f-832e-45518c109170" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7816 :tutorial/title "The Order in Which Transformations Are Applied"} :step/id #uuid "140e0b0f-05ee-520d-b270-851be2a46177" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093267}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092347 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd2b79a-bc49-5c01-9237-4642e237863f"} :step/id #uuid "1e9d2b73-4f18-544f-a849-ee44bb39f8a0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092348 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad47b548-a298-5cc9-8f69-70fee292db5d"} :step/id #uuid "2a682d54-8422-59c1-a71e-fbf5456518d6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b16f563c-f4be-5883-8734-cbce48de4757"} :step/id #uuid "73b04d52-8750-5022-91fd-57c8d22e4cc7"}] :activity/title "Combining Linear Transformations Using 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092432}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050646 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092347 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091940 :question/id #uuid "61044ed5-5b73-5c83-a9fa-182af6de775c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12538" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation that acts on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ as follows (in the order given):\n\n- it rotates vectors by $90^\\circ$ clockwise about the origin, and then\n\n- stretches the result by a scale factor of $2$ in the $y$-direction.\n\nWhat is the image of the unit square shown below under the action of this linear transformation?\n\n![](images/e9/e966d34d56ae25deb6bbf5849e813493c51730981031955e6833fd04d9ec1e2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the rotation of $90^\\circ$ clockwise:\n\n![](images/4d/4da9df5bb1487aa693d9301c4a4901394da332197328637deef671a2bc59d204.png)\n\nThen, we apply the stretch by a scale factor of $2$ in the $y$-direction:\n\n![](images/f4/f400e267f38f4624d64e3a1899f9bbed3ba69996f1cf846ccc59e06e9eb5f35e.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd2b79a-bc49-5c01-9237-4642e237863f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "320d09c2-6de6-5ed6-a14b-3614b9254114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f400e267f38f4624d64e3a1899f9bbed3ba69996f1cf846ccc59e06e9eb5f35e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336507} {:answer/id #uuid "4b36f438-85de-5751-acd2-5f818620eb44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/326fd93c3494c23a2ae97297964d94732c36a9aabf6c5b9d2d2647d6626fbf44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336728} {:answer/id #uuid "5e310520-67fe-51a7-b30b-aa0dd845790f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer