he system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2} &=4 \\\\ 0 &=2k-10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=2k-10,$ which means that $k=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f479d1d-5d75-5f3c-8153-6a84de5b7ca8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408140} {:answer/id #uuid "be5b0ebc-829b-5a60-838a-1ec9ecacfa60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408151} {:answer/id #uuid "ec6eff0c-554b-5fe5-937d-c0a3c886c493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408154} {:answer/id #uuid "fb94d986-309a-566f-aca1-f08bcb7d0e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408156} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6f84ec-14e8-58fa-9f02-c1ec2a769c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408156} :answer-field/id #uuid "15e1383f-1b6d-5090-8b4e-e602188986c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408158}] :question/id #uuid "5304c962-1643-582f-be3c-68ed826e56d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110975" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ k+5\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 7 & -2 & k+5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-7{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+40\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=-5 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 0 &=k+30.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+30,$ which means that $k=-30.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25070fd0-c7a7-5aad-88d7-0112a5cceca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429983} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcf3ee9-518e-59ca-a87d-eb7fff848801" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429984} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2ca576-5a4b-51b9-8394-245a07fc1667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429987} {:answer/id #uuid "80756020-169b-5fd6-8215-6076a6443964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429990} {:answer/id #uuid "909a8f13-1f9a-5602-b685-461ec6c51d98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429987} :answer-field/id #uuid "35749f45-4831-5cfb-b5cd-f36d9fb901de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429997}] :question/id #uuid "1df900d6-620c-58af-b345-1b8940d52367" :question/math-academy-id "q-110981" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ -14 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ -1 & 1 & k\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 4 & k-20\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -20 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k+8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=-20 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-14 \\\\ 0 &=k+8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k+8,$ which means that $k=-8.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af634ac-2adf-5266-9d5e-f4ec6782ebb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480817} {:answer/id #uuid "6a7caeaf-072f-52ba-8fa7-2bd619dc7e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480937} {:answer/id #uuid "b3cc978a-23f8-5e0c-a899-6cecd8ec9666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481082} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ba0f1e-64d5-5b98-bd5a-3378ee864a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481121} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fc9398-1cd8-5d45-bef7-2d3eb160e0af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480817} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f12123e-d897-5bb5-a021-652c5ffc786d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481283}] :question/id #uuid "379e6627-cf03-580e-9f81-1437be9f7987" :question/math-academy-id "q-110978" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ k+30\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ then the equation $Ax=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 21 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & k+30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} &=12 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=7 \\\\ 0 &=k-5.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-5,$ which means that $k=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191150d3-af6c-5369-8a94-a6bfdd39b6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480790} {:answer/id #uuid "95981581-0e25-501f-b8a4-5a0f6cf1319e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481023} {:answer/id #uuid "ad84d374-04e9-548c-b3e0-df1326473bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481064} {:answer/id #uuid "d3299a9a-dd1e-5fcf-906a-3fe1e9c9e612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481134} {:answer/id #uuid "db545c16-037d-5d5e-bd83-57a3b42fa7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481064} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a46153c-e7f9-5a37-81cc-90b7f7320e59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481270}] :question/id #uuid "66f9559c-8f7e-590d-8f2d-e6935f863326" :question/math-academy-id "q-74210" :question/problem "Consider the matrix given by $C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\text{Col}(C).$" :question/worked-solution "If $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ then the equation $Cx=b$ must be consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -4 & k\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & k-4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & k-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ -4{x}_{2} &=6 \\\\ 0 &=k-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nIn order for this system to be consistent, we require $0=k-1,$ which means that $k=1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given a Vector That’s in the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1850 :topic/title "The Column Space of a Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134709} :activity/id #uuid "bff21f91-25dc-5812-8a0d-35d914692a82" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135064 :tutorial/content "When carrying out a linear regression in two variables, say $x$ and $y,$ our task is to find a straight line that best fits a set of data points $(x_i,y_i).$ We can easily extend this technique to situations involving three variables. \n\nThe following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by a small company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nSince we now have three variables, $x_1, x_2,$ and $y,$ our task is to find a *plane* of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data.\n\n![](images/ce/ced34ebf098fd20194fafd74f4f75bf855707ad1457e706264c9e922a7efa706.png)\n\n  \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}+2{\\beta }_{2}=6 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+3{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+4{\\beta }_{2}=18 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+6{\\beta }_{2}=24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAs usual, $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the coefficient vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nSince our points do not all lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0,$ $\\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the plane that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the plane of best fit (regression plane) is \n\n$$\ny = 2+ 7x_1-x_2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNote that we can use the same technique to fit data involving more variables. For example, if we have four variables $x_1, x_2, x_3,$ and $y,$ then we can find a four-dimensional **hyperplane** of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0+ \\beta_1x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2 + \\beta_3 x_3\n$$\n\nthat best fits a set of data points $(x_{1i},x_{2i}, x_{3i}, y).$  The coefficients of this hyperplane are found by solving the corresponding least-squares problem. \n\nFinally, applying linear regression to situations involving three or more variables is called **multiple linear regression**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac48d158-e2b0-57f9-b8ba-6fe587ca807a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "958cc7d8-671b-5ebd-9b9d-c938658e82bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae"} :step/id #uuid "9b6349ce-1de8-5cd8-aed5-641cee35b3ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134891}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eaee4e9-7be5-5c35-bc88-89697a15d75a"} :step/id #uuid "2aaa8ea5-3420-58b2-86f9-e413b60cb5d4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134891 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135145 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might need to place a constraint on the regression plane that forces it to pass through the origin. \n\nLet's once again consider our small company. The following table shows the number of units $x_1$ and $x_2$ (in hundreds) of two goods produced by the company in several production runs and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) obtained by the company.   \n\n| Good Type 1 $(x_1)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Good Type 2 $(x_2)$ | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$6$** |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $6$ | $15$ | $18$ | $24$ |\n\nIt's known that the profit equals zero when the company does not produce any goods, so $y=0$ when $x_1=x_2 = 0.$ The company wishes to find a plane that best fits the data *subject to* the constraint that it should pass through the point $(0,0, 0).$\n\nTherefore, we want to find the plane\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\nSubstituting the values of $x_1$ and $x_2$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 15 \\\\ 18 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nThe rest of the arguments are the same as before. Since not all points lie on a plane, there will be no parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_1$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e7ff642-aa4a-5099-ac22-42297dcfcacf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6153 :tutorial/title "Regression Planes Passing Through the Origin"} :step/id #uuid "4adafa4a-1969-530d-9be3-b8157c980149" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134711}}] :activity/title "Multiple Linear Regression With Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134031}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050684 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133617 :question/id #uuid "18e10da5-f3e1-52d2-b2c6-2a69a1bf1947" :question/math-academy-id "e-5614" :question/problem "| $x_1$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$7$** | **$6.5$** | **$6$** | **$5$** | **$3$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n![](images/9d/9da2a49a4bab645fa1b8ea3f98e6aa6135b191e121d0cc78a73b517fbc510fc0.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Find  $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context." :question/worked-solution "| $x_1$ | **$\\color{blue}1$** | **$\\color{blue}2$** | **$\\color{blue}3$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** | **$\\color{blue}5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x_2$ | **$\\color{purple}7$** | **$\\color{purple}6.5$** | **$\\color{purple}6$** | **$\\color{purple}5$** | **$\\color{purple}3$** |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}5$ | $\\color{red}9$ | $\\color{red}15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\beta_2 x_2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nThus, we have\n![](images/b5/b5302251a49a45514a55d2987289548e2a55917884ff4c240be081ba512a970e.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e33c97-0499-5829-9886-be30b00989ae" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134113 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f3e4765-c972-5c92-ae94-1d96fc94ce26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 15 \\\\ 4 & 18 \\\\ 5 & 22\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130835} {:answer/id #uuid "a78ed443-f2e7-50db-9e7b-ff238d69c346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 15 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 18 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 5 & 22 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130836} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a0888c-fdf8-5a37-bfee-7341b067127e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130837} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe1b304-8d0a-5d8f-94ba-3f94be937f46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer