60e-08535cff4002" :question/math-academy-id "q-159734" :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$7$** | **$3$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$8$** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$19$** | **$22$** |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ | $-10$ |\n\n  \n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$10$** | **$8$** | **$7$** | **$3$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$8$** | **$12$** | **$14$** | **$19$** | **$22$** |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ | $-10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 15 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 10 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 14 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 19 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 22\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of a Multiple Linear Regression That Passes Through the Origin"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2171 :topic/title "Multiple Linear Regression With Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229982} :activity/id #uuid "ecc6f7d5-a6a0-5418-9648-573635651982" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230191 :tutorial/content "Matrix exponentials can be used for solving *inhomogeneous systems* of ODEs as well. Consider the system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant square matrix, and suppose we are given an initial condition $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$.\n\nOur goal is to derive the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ of the system.\n\nFirst, we eliminate the *inhomogeneous term* by a change of variables. We define\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nand differentiate it using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =\\cancel{-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+\\cancel{A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)}+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate both sides from $t_0$ to $t$ and get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNext, since $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to the initial variable $\\mathbf{x}(t).$ To do that we multiply both sides by $e^{A(t-t_0)}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\ x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})-A(s-{t}_{0})}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x({t}_{0})+{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our IVP is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere the first term represents the solution of the corresponding *homogeneous system*, and the second term gives a particular solution of our *inhomogeneous system*.\n\nLet's see some concrete examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "ff1bfa7f-286f-5481-8170-effabae1aa53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14524 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2e2fe55d-42e2-5c03-89a2-e19a648504d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229983}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229983 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8"} :step/id #uuid "77745a11-4271-51f5-b398-2f10162b46cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229984}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229984 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b"} :step/id #uuid "6314ffb3-c936-5a5a-b8eb-f9a041a7e1a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229985}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229985 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229133 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04"} :step/id #uuid "96c720fc-5ade-5920-b439-4021bbbc0586"}] :activity/title "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229257} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495193} :activity/id #uuid "b9d3e9cf-c6b8-5144-84b6-fb2bd0e11696" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495181 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495161 :question/id #uuid "e9f770d5-4f03-58ec-b9ae-bcbaf7705c59"} :step/id #uuid "06d91f07-0fea-5beb-aa8a-cd77b3e6a896" :step/next {:db/id